# Fields

A field is data. That is the whole premise of this card, and most of what
follows is a consequence of it.

## There is no field visibility

HQL has no `pub`, `protected` or `private`. Nothing in a type is hidden from
anyone holding a value of it, and a subtype neither changes the visibility of an
inherited field nor conceals one.

The reason is not minimalism. Visibility modifiers exist to protect an
**inside**: a privileged execution context — methods on the object — that may
touch representation the outside must not. HQL has no such context. Values are
data, declared fields are queryable data, and every consumer of a value is in the
same position as every other. A modifier that partitions callers into privileged
and unprivileged would be partitioning a set with one member.

So the question a visibility system answers — *may I reach this?* — is not a
question HQL has. The question it does have is *is this here?*, and that is what
`?` is for.

## Required and optional fields

A field declared without `?` is **required** by that type. A field declared with
`?` **may be absent**.

```hql
type Doc {
    header : Data
    body   : Content
}

type HmdHeader {
    metadata? : Data
}
```

Every `Doc` has a header and a body; asking for either always succeeds. An
`HmdHeader` may or may not carry `metadata`, because a document does not have to
author any.

This is an ordinary part of the type system, not a device for emulating
something else. Most real schemas have optional parts — a `nickname`, a `born`
date, a config block a deployment may not set — and `?` is how a type says so
once, at the declaration, instead of every reader guessing.

### Absence is not a weaker value

`?` says the field may not be there. It does not say the field is there holding
nothing. HQL has no null, and absence is carried by `Option` — see [[option-type]] — so
a reader of an optional field gets something it must handle rather than
something that might surprise it.

Whether `metadata? : Data` is therefore exactly `metadata : Option<Data>`, or
whether a missing key in a tree and a present key holding `None` stay
distinguishable, is open. So is the relationship to the postfix spelling
`born: Date?` used on [[card-type]]: one marks the *field* optional and the other
marks the *type* optional, and they may or may not be the same claim.

## Partial schemas over `Data`

[[data-type|Data]] is an open tree whose keys belong to whoever wrote them, and
it stays that way. What a type may do is declare a **partial schema**: a shape
for the paths it knows about, leaving every other path untouched and still open.

```hql
type HmdDoc <: Doc {
    header : HmdHeader        // a known shape on some paths
}
```

`HmdHeader` constrains `metadata` and says nothing about the rest, so a vault
inventing `deploy.region` tomorrow breaks nothing and needs no declaration. This
is the same progressive refinement [[type-system]] describes, written down in
advance rather than established at a use site: a type can supply the known paths
up front, and a program can still establish more about a particular value later.

The two meet without conflict because neither closes the tree. A schema that
required the whole tree to be specified statically would make `Data` useless for
the thing it exists for.

## The worked example: document metadata and card metadata

This is where the distinction that matters most in the knowledge domain lives,
and it is worth stating slowly, because two similar-looking paths mean different
things.

```hql
doc.header              // always present
doc.header.metadata?    // may be absent — authored, or not
card.metadata           // always present — assembled
```

**The header is not optional and is never hidden.** Every document has one, and
every holder of a document can read it. Most of it is derived — name, path,
format — so there is nothing for an author to omit.

**`header.metadata` is optional**, because authoring metadata is a choice.

**`card.metadata` is a different field**, not a renaming, an alias, or the same
field reached another way. When a document becomes a [[card-type|Card]], the card
gets a metadata field of its own, and that field is *assembled*. Its inputs
include:

- the authored `header.metadata`, if the document has one;
- system metadata the runtime supplies at load;
- entries contributed by extensions — `git`, an indexer, a link checker;
- vault state held under `.hmd/**`;
- semantic-search state and other derived information.

Authored metadata participates in that assembly under the Card metadata rules,
including their defined precedence. It is one input among several, and being
authored does not by itself win — see [[card-type]].

### Two layers, and a query has to know which

Both fields remain addressable, and they answer different questions:

| Path | Question it answers |
| --- | --- |
| `card.header.metadata` | what did the author of this file write? |
| `card.metadata` | what does the system know about this card? |

A query over `card.header.metadata` sees authored intent and nothing else. A
query over `card.metadata` sees the effective picture and cannot tell, without
asking further, which layer any given entry came from.

Neither is the right answer in general. A linter checking that authors tagged
their notes wants the first; a search ranking cards wants the second; and a tool
that confuses them will report the vault's own bookkeeping as something a person
typed. The complexity is real and belongs in the open: a document has metadata,
and the card built from it has its own.

## Open questions

- **Whether `field? : T` is exactly `field : Option<T>`**, and whether an absent
  key stays distinguishable from a present key holding `None`.
- **Whether `metadata? : Data` and `born: Date?` are one feature or two** — the
  field marked optional versus the type marked optional.
- **Whether a subtype may change optionality**, making a required field optional
  or an optional field required, and in which direction that is sound.
- **What a partial schema does with a path that is present but ill-typed** — a
  diagnostic, an absence, or a value carrying its failure.
- **The precedence rules for Card metadata assembly**, which this card names and
  [[card-type]] owns. Which inputs may overwrite which, and whether an entry
  records the layer it came from, are not settled.
- **Whether `card.metadata` is materialized at load or computed on demand**,
  which matters once assembly has cost.

See [[type-system]] for subtyping, generics and refinement, [[data-type]] for the
tree a partial schema constrains, [[doc-type]] for the header, [[card-type]] for
the assembled metadata and its rules, and [[hql/design-status]] for what remains
undecided.
